{\shptxt\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li1285\sl240\slmult1\sb13\f1\fs20\b0\cf6 online.unimelb.edu.au\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li20\sl240\slmult1\sb130\f1\fs20\b0\cf6 \uc1\u61667 ? Copyright University of \f1\fs20\expnd-1\expndtw-4 Melbourne\par}}}
\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl14\slmult1\fs20\par}\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li155\qc\sl240\slmult1\sb221\tx3741\tx10848{\*\bkmkstart Video transcript}{\*\bkmkend Video transcript}\f0\fs40\b0\cf1{\ul  \tab \f3\b1\expnd4\expndtw21 Video\expnd13\expndtw63  \expnd5\expndtw26 transcript\tab }\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li150\qc\sl240\slmult1\sb231{\*\bkmkstart Information Assurance}{\*\bkmkend Information Assurance}\f3\fs26\b1\cf2 Information Assurance\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f3\fs9\b1\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\sl240\slmult1\sb93{\*\bkmkstart Risk in Information Security}{\*\bkmkend Risk in Information Security}\f3\fs24\b1\cf4 Risk in Information Security\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb5\f3\fs31\b1\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri1032\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Shedden, P. & Ahmad, A. (2017). \f2\i1 Risk in Information Security \f1\i0 [Video file]. University of Melbourne. Retrieved from: {\field {\fldinst { HYPERLINK "https://gomelb.unimelb.edu.au/" }}{\fldrslt \cf5{\ul https://gomelb.unimelb.edu.au}}
}\cf5 {\ul }\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb2\f1\fs23\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri877\sl240\slmult1\sb94\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: Welcome Dr. Piya Shedden to this conversation on information security risk management. So my question for you is, how well does industry manage information security risk in your opinion?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: Risk is a really challenging topic for organisations, big organisation especially so. So, there are certain industry trends at the moment where data protection and knowing what data information your organisation actually has in order to protect it is coming up as sort of number one in quite a few research reports. And the second is regulatory compliance, which is always our favourite topic.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 The challenges come through where risks in organisations are heavily distributed across multiple business units and the technology silos, I guess, that supports each of those business units. And the fact that there's also the perception amongst business folk that cybersecurity risks are not necessarily owned by the business even though they feel the impacts, but it's just a technology problem.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri652\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 And then conversely as well. You have a whole bunch of very talented technical individuals operating security controls, for example, but they may not actually know the nature of the data that resides on a server that they're actually hardening, for example. And so where you don't have that innate sort of understanding of the data classification, of the data retained on the server, the availability requirements and whether there are any data quality concerns, for instance, it can actually make it quite difficult to typify the risk profile holistically of that information or that assit, and then elevate that up for management.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Furthermore, the actual risk structures can actually get very complex. So we're not thinking of organisations where they may have 1, 2, or 3 key systems-- and there are organisations that also have risk-based challenges. But in banks, in telecommunications, et cetera, we're talking 3,000, 5,000, 10,000, 20,000 individual systems or clusters of assets. And as you can imagine, that makes the ability and the capability to appropriately assess and then manage risk and security risk of those enterprises a massive undertaking.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: My question to you is, one of the big criticisms that's been levelled against mismanagement is that risk assessments aren't done frequently enough. What's your experience with that?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri516\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: I'd say, especially in regulated organisations-- so I'm thinking financial services again-- risk assessments are actually performed quite frequently. And that's because due to the nature of the regulator-- in this case, APRA-- they've actually put quite stringent risk-based requirements, I guess, on organisations if they actually want to maintain a banking licence, for instance, or operate as an insurer.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 So in those kinds of environments where it's kind of stipulated, so pushed, I guess, by the regulator and pulled in those circumstances where due diligence and doing the right thing by a customer applies, risk assessments are actually conducted reasonably frequently in relation to the degree of risk. That's actually a really interesting point that you raised as well because as technology operations become more agile, the change becomes a lot more frequent in the desire for new products, for instance, by customers, I guess the risk world also needs to start keeping up with that.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f1\fs20\b0 And so then we find those other organisations in technology, some retailers, for example, that may not operate at that same sort of frequency with that same sort of attention paid to risk even though their operate technology and business operational environments do change, not to mention the fact that the threat world, the security threat world, changes every single moment of every single day.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li765\sl240\slmult1\sb185\f2\fs20\b0\i1\cf3 Current as of: June, 2017.\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery1\footery982\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb5\f2\fs10\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\sb94\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: You've seen a lot of these risk assessments and you might have conducted many of them yourself. What is the intent behind the risk assessment? Is it really investigative or is it really about compliance and just a tick the box exercise?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: It links back to organisational risk and security culture, I guess. So there are retail organisations, for example, that I have consulted into where they really did have a customer first and a customer care type mentality. And that was amazing to watch.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Some technology organisations as well that provide specific services to members of the public, for example, they're actually quite diligent in wanting to ensure that they protect the data that matters most. A lot of the time though it is compliance driven. So the Australian public is a lot more aware after the 2014 data privacy amendments that came through. And therefore, people are a lot more conscious, I guess, of how organisations are actually using the data and what people are doing with it and where it goes.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri568\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Any time that you swipe a credit card or you input \expnd-1\expndtw-3 it \expnd0\expndtw0 on to a steam gaming or something like that or buy that jacket online or a monster truck on eBay-- I'm just making things up now. But ultimately, anything they \expnd-1\expndtw-3 use \expnd0\expndtw0 as a credit card transaction needs to comply with PCI-DSS. But again, that's compliance driven and the fear of fines and penalties and whatnot.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri587\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 But when you actually dig down into \expnd-1\expndtw-3 it \expnd0\expndtw0 it's also about reputation and the impact on customer and the impact on trust. So \expnd-1\expndtw-3 it \expnd0\expndtw0 is a bit of a push from \expnd-1\expndtw-3 that \expnd0\expndtw0 regulatory perspective and linking back to, again, \expnd-1\expndtw-3 it \expnd0\expndtw0 is a bit of a pull from the fact that a \expnd-1\expndtw-3 lot \expnd0\expndtw0 of organisations are looking to do the right things by their\expnd-7\expndtw-36  \expnd0\expndtw0 customers.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: I like the fact that you use the word fear. So I'm going to ask you this question. How much of the risk that you see in organisations is driven by perception and how much of it is driven by actual risk?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri576\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: So when actually looking at sort of the threat environment out there to say, well, what could actually impact on our asset, a lot of it actually comes down to the nature of the asset and the value of the asset. And I guess that could be perceived value as well. But if it's got sensitive data and it's a business critical service that operates on that infrastructure, for example, or that applicational platform, we sort of start-- and this is the art of risk assessment-- you start to piece together that view of what the asset actually is and its importance in the company or organisation, sorry, and what happens if it's actually breached or if it goes down.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri607\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 So the way that organisations perceive a threat, for instance, it is perception. But there's levels of sophistication in the Australian market that are getting up there in terms of knowing what the actual threats are that may impact our environment and being able to triage the real ones in and keep the, I guess, the perceived bad ones out. But then along come these amazing events like we've seen recently where it was all nobody saw it coming because of incredibly sophisticated malware attack brought about through social engineering.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri1032\sl532\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: We were talking about the WannaCry ransomeware that's been running around last weekend. DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: That's correct. Yes\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri587\sl240\slmult1\sb7\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: So I guess the intent behind my question was, with senior managers, especially those that are not directly involved in security, where do they get their security risk perceptions from? Is it from the newspaper? Is it from a friend? Or is that from, indeed, their own security managers?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: The Australian community, I guess-- we've always had that view of Australia being the lucky country. It's out there. It's all the way down to the south and east and we're quite far away from everything and nothing possibly could ever happen here.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 But over the last couple of years, it's actually been quite noticeable seeing senior management-- we're talking the executive and the board-- realise that they are actually personally accountable in order to appropriately manage cybersecurity risk and enforce their security control environment. And it takes the nice big breeches, I guess, unfortunately with the likes of the retailer in the US and a few online services where millions of credit card numbers were\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs21\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li765\sl240\slmult1\sb94\f2\fs20\b0\i1\cf3 Current as of: June, 2017.\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery1\footery982
