\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl14\slmult1\fs20\par}\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb5\f2\fs10\b0\i1\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri1032\sl240\slmult1\sb94\f1\fs20\b0 actually released and/or the personal information, some health information leaked externally. You get these big breaches and it really wakes the exec up.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri877\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Where do they get this information from? Well, there's a lot of delegation that goes in, but their concern comes from seeing their peers out in the market being impacted by cyber events. They see what's happening in the media, and that's why they have dedicated security teams to be able to sift through and determine what is actually right for the organisation to mitigate and what may actually not present a real risk to the organisation.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: So let me ask you this question. What are the key drivers for security investment in an organisation, especially when you are talking about the CEO? What drives a CEO to spend money on information security?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri569\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: I'd sort of classify it into three big buckets, I guess. And the first one I'll start off with because risk is so close to my heart-- wouldn't it be amazing if all decisions were made on an intense data-driven or evidence-driven risk-based judgement? So where I've seen security communities and security teams, I guess, raising up requirements or business cases, I guess, for IT spend and investment, that's typically been on the back of various risk-based judgments.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri587\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 We're seeing data around increased-- and we're talking data analytics capabilities, for example-- we're seeing evidence that these are the risks that will be highlighted in 2017, for instance, or will be increasing on the horizon based on the data that we've been able to collect or on the trends that we've seen in the past. So those types of judgments are actually made and they do tend to determine or at least attempt to document the risk reduction of a time if they do implement these controls.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri877\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 Then, of course, there's breaches. So there's a breach. We've been hurt. Something has happened. Something has gotten out into the ether or we've suffered a series of major outages over the last 2 or 3 weeks.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 That typically drives a lot of spend because that, especially for big organisations, gets them into the front page. It robs you of customer confidence and the faces of the firm, ultimately, they're the ones that are in front of the media having to explain why their core services are out or why there has been a major data breach.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri748\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 And finally, I guess, it's a regulatory compliance angle. So organisations are spending a lot of money, for instance, still in this day and age years after it was implemented to uplift their security control environment in line with the PCI-DSS, which I've mentioned before, the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard. 600 or so control requirements, people, process, and technology.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri553\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 A lot of organisations are still requiring that uplift. And that's still something that we help organisations do because these programmes can take years. And funnily enough, the requirements change from time to time so certain technologies are no longer admissible under the standards so some organisations, therefore, need to implement new technologies in order to meet those obligations or requirements or because their technology or their business environment changes.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 And so the security control environment needs to step up or change to meet those needs.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri575\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: So I guess if I was a security manager, the thing that I'm thinking about most is, how do I get my CEO to spend money or give me the resources I need? So in your opinion, how does a security manager then make a case for resources if the senior manager or the senior managers are in a sense driven by either response to a breach or new legislation?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: Excellent. Ultimately, it comes down to risk. And I guess the business cases that I've seen circulated really do-- for seeking remediation funding, for instance, or investment for future initiatives, it does come down to risk and the risk justification. So we are seeing this trend.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri652\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 This introduces this risk to the company. We're basing it on this evidence. And therefore, these are the specific attacks or the tech vectors that will open up. This is the change in the threat environment, which will essentially manifest and impact our organisation. These security controls are not operating as effectively as they could be to mitigate that.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri1032\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 We basically mix it up into a big risk bucket and present that as part of the case. And there are various models and things that are used out there. Ultimately, it is formal documentation. Ideally, it should be in line with the security\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery1\footery982\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb5\f1\fs10\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri536\sl240\slmult1\sb94\f1\fs20\b0 strategy and the IT and the organisational strategy because that will ensure that the right people will at least see that it' s your business case is in alignment into the direction that you want to move.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 And I have seen, I guess, other organisations that have a bucket of funding, I guess, as part of a broad enterprise programme off to the side. So it gets funded every year and then part of that, I guess, investment committee makes decisions on which specific ordered items we want to close this year, for example. Or if that goes into the business case bucket because ordered items are kind of mandatory things you need to shut down, right?\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri532\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 But for other big risky items, risky platforms, and those sorts of things, I have seen investment into those information as it's driven by a more programme and project level funding, which can come and can go depending on the profits and the health of the organisation.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f1\fs24\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\ri3140\sl532\slmult1\f1\fs20\b0 DR. ATIF AHMAD: Thank you very much for your responses to these questions on risk. DR. PIYA SHEDDEN: No worries.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\li710\sl240\slmult1\sb7\f1\fs20\b0 END TIMECODE: 13.32\par}